window.docContents[134].resolve({"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Monad-Transformers--A-Monad-Construction-Kit":{"contents":" is far from the only useful monad transformer.\nThis section describes a number of additional transformers.\nEach monad transformer consists of the following:\n\n1. A definition or datatype  that takes a monad as an argument.\n    It should have a type like , though it may accept additional arguments prior to the monad.2. A  instance for  that relies on an instance of . This enables the transformed monad to be used as a monad.3. A  instance that translates actions of type  into actions of type , for arbitrary monads . This enables actions from the underlying monad to be used in the transformed monad.\n\nFurthermore, the  instance for the transformer should obey the contract for , at least if the underlying  instance does.\nIn addition,  should be equivalent to  in the transformed monad, and  should distribute over  so that  is the same as .\n\nMany monad transformers additionally define type classes in the style of  that describe the actual effects available in the monad.\nThis can provide more flexibility: it allows programs to be written that rely only on an interface, and don't constrain the underlying monad to be implemented by a given transformer.\nThe type classes are a way for programs to express their requirements, and monad transformers are a convenient way to meet these requirements.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Monad Transformers","header":"6.2. A Monad Construction Kit","id":"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Monad-Transformers--A-Monad-Construction-Kit"}});